<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="namespaces" title="Namespaces and identifiers">

    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>
            Assumes a hand-built weaver — a namespace only matters to a plugin loaded that way.
            <a href="what-a-plugin-can-do.topic">What a plugin can do</a> has why.
        </p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        Every identifier a <code>WeaverPlugin</code> contributes is spelled
        <code>namespace:name</code>. The namespace is yours alone, and the colon is what lets a
        diagnostic say whose contribution failed.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["Plugin owns the namespace acme"] --> B["Factory offers acme:AFTER_LOGGING"]
    B --> C{"Prefixed with its owner?"}
    C -->|no| D["The whole factory is dropped"]
    C -->|yes| E["Held in the registry"]
    E --> F["A weave names an identifier"]
    F --> G{"Registered under that spelling?"}
    G -->|yes| H["The contribution answers"]
    G -->|no| I{"Retired under an alias?"}
    I -->|yes| H
    I -->|no| J["Resolves to nothing"]
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="What a name is made of" id="parts">
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="The namespace">
                <p>
                    One lowercase word your plugin declares in its <code>PluginId</code>. Where a
                    plugin comes from at all is
                    <a href="what-a-plugin-can-do.topic">What a plugin can do</a>.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The identifier">
                <p>
                    Your namespace, a colon, then at least one character. An injector kind
                    allows no second colon; an injection point does — the grammar below has
                    both.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The built-in namespace">
                <p>
                    The empty one, which is why <code>HEAD</code> carries no colon.
                    <a href="injection-points.topic">Injection points</a> has the spellings it
                    holds.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="An alias">
                <p>
                    A retired spelling and the current one behind it. Both resolve to the same
                    contribution, but the spelling you wrote is handed straight to the factory
                    and folded into the plan's fingerprint, so it changes the stamp on the class
                    even though it changes nothing about what runs.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="The grammar" id="grammar">
        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Part</td>
                <td>Must be</td>
                <td>Refused</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Namespace</td>
                <td><code>[a-z][a-z0-9-]*</code></td>
                <td><code>Acme</code>, <code>acme_tools</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>aether</code></td>
                <td>reserved, never a plugin's</td>
                <td><code>aether</code>, as a namespace or a registration</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Empty namespace</td>
                <td>the framework's own</td>
                <td>any plugin that claims it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Identifier</td>
                <td><code>acme:</code> plus a character</td>
                <td><code>AFTER_LOG</code>, <code>acme:</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Injector kind</td>
                <td>one colon, text either side</td>
                <td><code>acme:a:b</code>, <code>:trace</code></td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            Nothing here is folded, trimmed or normalised. A name off the grammar is refused
            rather than corrected. An alias applies no such check: nothing stops a plugin
            retiring an unregistered <code>aether:</code> spelling in its favour.
        </p>

        <p>
            A metadata key is the one thing the namespace is applied to instead of checked
            against. <code>metadata("mode", "strict")</code> from <code>acme</code> is recorded as
            <code>acme:mode</code>, and every key a plugin records feeds the plan's fingerprint.
            <a href="pipeline.topic">The weaving pipeline</a> says what a fingerprint that moves
            costs you.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Which failure you meet" id="failures">
        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="A namespace off the grammar">
                <p>
                    <code>new PluginId("Acme", ...)</code> throws
                    <code>IllegalArgumentException</code>, so your plugin never exists to be
                    loaded. Spell the namespace as the table above does.
                    <code>AW3100</code> covers the same ground for a registry assembled by hand,
                    and no loaded plugin can reach it —
                    <a href="diagnostics-policy.topic">the policy and plugin codes</a> list where
                    each one fires.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The name aether">
                <p>
                    The same constructor and the same exception. <code>aether</code> satisfies the
                    pattern and is caught by the check after it, so the message you get names the
                    reservation rather than the shape. Choose another word.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="An identifier outside your namespace">
                <p>
                    Reported instead of thrown: <code>AW3110</code>, and the factory that offered
                    it is dropped whole rather than partly registered. Prefix every identifier the
                    factory names with your own namespace.
                    <a href="reporting.topic">Report rather than throw</a> has the convention behind
                    that split.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A plugin claiming the empty namespace">
                <p>
                    Refused at a load gate as <code>AW3101</code>, before it is asked to
                    contribute anything; give it a namespace of its own.
                    <a href="plugin-lifecycle.topic">The plugin lifecycle</a> has the order the gates
                    run in.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Two plugins, one namespace" id="collision">
        <p>
            A namespace has exactly one owner per weaver. The second claim is
            <code>AW3111</code>, quoting both identities and both classes. That plugin
            contributes nothing — drop one of the two jars, or ask an author to rename.
        </p>

        <p>
            Which of the two loses never varies with the classpath. Candidates are sorted by
            namespace, then by implementation class name, before the first gate sees any of
            them. Two sharing both keep the order they arrived in.
            <a href="determinism.topic">Determinism</a> covers why that matters beyond this page.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="An alias is checked less than a registration" id="aliases">
        <p>
            The registry checks a registered identifier against the namespace that registered it.
            It checks neither half of an alias against a namespace. Three rules apply instead:
        </p>

        <list>
            <li><p>Declaring the same deprecated spelling twice with different replacements is
                refused as <code>AW3111</code> as soon as it is declared, keeping the later
                one.</p></li>
            <li><p>Once the registry is built, the replacement must be registered. Otherwise
                <code>AW3121</code>, listing what was available — register it, or correct the
                alias.</p></li>
            <li><p>At the same point, the retired spelling must not also be registered.
                Otherwise <code>AW3111</code>; an identifier is current or retired, and you
                drop whichever of the two you did not mean.</p></li>
        </list>

        <p>
            So an alias may retire an unqualified spelling that no namespace owns, and the
            diagnostics it produces name no plugin. A lookup consults the registered identifiers
            first, which is why a retiring alias can never take a built-in spelling away from
            the framework.
        </p>

        <note>
            <p>
                Resolving through an alias reports <code>AW3120</code> to the listener the lookup
                was made with, and the weaver makes both of its lookups with one that discards.
                A retired spelling therefore weaves silently. Read a plugin's own release notes to
                learn that a spelling was retired; there is nothing to change in the build to make
                the warning appear.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

